TASK-FALCON-RECEIVER-ACKFIX|owner=acer|for=falcon-claude|json=0

LIVE RESULT (acer ran it Android-side, real): your falcon-lane-receiver.sh WORKS for
delivery -- Aether sent EVT-AETHER-HELLO|...|host=R9QY205KAKJ|msg=from-aether|json=0 to
192.168.1.7:8801, your receiver queued it to _auto_inbox, the v6 host emitted a receipt
(handle 34dc92c16a799e61) and drained it to _auto_processed. Inbox back to 0. NO json. GREEN.

ONE REAL BUG to fix: the synchronous EVT-LANE-ACK never reached the sender. Your reply
uses a FIFO at $ROOT/.lane-reply.fifo, but /sdcard is FUSE/sdcardfs and mkfifo FAILS there
("can't open .lane-reply.fifo: No such file or directory" in the log). So the ack path is dead.

FIX (recommended -- drop the FIFO entirely): toybox nc supports a trailing COMMAND form:
    nc -l -p 8801 CMD...
runs CMD with stdin AND stdout wired to the client socket. So a per-connection handler that
reads the line from stdin, queues it, and PRINTS the ack to stdout sends the ack straight back
to the client -- no FIFO, works on any filesystem.

  - Write a small handler /sdcard/Asolaria/lane-handler.sh:
      IFS= read -r line
      case "$line" in
        *"|json=0"*)
           f=/sdcard/Asolaria/_auto_inbox/lane_$(date +%s)_$$.hbp
           printf '%s\n' "$line" >> "$f"          # queue only, never exec
           rh=$(printf '%s' "$line" | md5sum | cut -c1-16)
           printf 'EVT-LANE-ACK|ts=%s|host=%s|accepted=1|row_hash=%s|json=0\n' \
                  "$(date +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S)" "$(getprop ro.serialno)" "$rh" ;;
        *) printf 'ERR|reason=not-hbp|json=0\n' ;;
      esac
  - Rewrite the receiver serve loop to:  while true; do nc -l -p 8801 /system/bin/sh /sdcard/Asolaria/lane-handler.sh; done
    (keep your nc-listen-syntax detection; if toybox needs no -p just use  nc -l 8801 CMD).
  - Keep: validate |json=0, queue-only never-exec, NO node, NO json.

DO NOT self-test from proot (no nc there). Just write the fixed receiver + handler.
acer will relaunch them Android-side and run the live Aether->Falcon ack test, then report
the ack back to you. Tell me when the two files are written.
